#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# The commit block, on a route that does not depend on the harness.
#
# `.claude/settings.json` already registers `scripts/gate-mechanical.sh` as a Claude Code
# `PreToolUse` hook. On 2026-08-13 that hook did not fire (V42) and four commits carrying a
# credential-shaped string landed; each was caught afterwards by `--report`, which is the wrong
# end of the process. The script was not at fault — invoked directly with a commit payload it
# blocks correctly, exit 2 — and neither was the configuration, which was executable, valid and
# unshadowed. The harness simply did not run it.
#
# **A hook that silently does not run looks exactly like a hook that passed.** That is the same
# shape as the always-red `credentials` row this episode began with, and it is why the guard
# lives here as well: git runs `pre-commit` itself, for every commit made in this repository,
# whatever issued it.
#
# ── WHY THE CLAUDECODE GUARD, AND WHY IT IS NOT A LOOPHOLE ────────────────────────────────────
#
# `scripts/gate-mechanical.sh` states the split this preserves, in its own header:
#
#     "The hook governs commits made through Claude Code only; a human committing from their
#      own terminal is unaffected. That is the intended split: the agent cannot decline, the
#      human is not blocked."
#
# `CLAUDECODE` is set in an agent's shell and absent from Julian's, so the two lines below are
# that sentence made executable. The asymmetry is deliberate: an agent needs a gate it cannot
# reason its way past, and a human needs to be able to commit a work in progress at 2am without
# arguing with a script.
#
# It is **not** an escape hatch, because nothing an agent can set turns it off — setting
# `CLAUDECODE` can only ever switch the gate *on*. The dangerous direction does not exist.
#
# ── INSTALLATION ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `.git/hooks/` is not version controlled, so this lives in a tracked directory and a clone
# runs one command:
#
#     git config core.hooksPath .githooks
#
# `test/git-hook.test.ts` asserts that config rather than skipping when it is absent, for the
# same reason `test/privilege-planes.test.ts` refuses to skip an unset DSN — an unwired guard
# that reports green is indistinguishable from one that works.
#
# ── NOTE ON `--no-verify` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `git commit --no-verify` bypasses this, as it bypasses every pre-commit hook. That is not a
# defect to be engineered around: it is the same category as "staging less, amending later, or
# narrowing a check", which `scripts/gate-mechanical.sh` names as the moves it exists to catch.
# The gate is a guard against forgetting, not against a determined bypass, and `--report` in
# `/check` remains the audit that sees everything regardless.

set -uo pipefail

# The human is not blocked. Nothing below runs, and no gate is consulted, for an ordinary
# terminal commit.
[ -n "${CLAUDECODE:-}" ] || exit 0

ROOT=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel) || exit 0

# Hook mode reads the tool call on stdin and does nothing unless the payload names a commit —
# see the dispatch at the top of the script. Feeding it a synthetic payload is how a git hook
# reaches the same code path the Claude Code hook would, so there is one implementation and the
# two routes cannot disagree about what passing means.
#
# `exec`, so the script's own exit status is the hook's: 2 blocks, and git treats any non-zero
# as a refusal. Its block message goes to stderr and reaches the terminal unchanged.
exec "$ROOT/scripts/gate-mechanical.sh" <<'PAYLOAD'
{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"git commit"}}
PAYLOAD
